```
# ============================================================================
# Detection: Bulk Active Directory Account/Group Enumeration
# Detection ID: DE-DI-001
# MITRE ATT&CK: T1087 - Account Discovery / T1069 - Permission Groups Discovery
# Data Source: Sysmon EventCode=1
# Severity: Low
# ============================================================================

index=endpoint sourcetype="XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational" EventCode=1
Image IN ("*\\net.exe","*\\net1.exe","*\\dsquery.exe","*\\whoami.exe","*\\nltest.exe")
CommandLine="*group*" OR CommandLine="*/domain*" OR CommandLine="*user*"
| stats count min(_time) as first_seen dc(CommandLine) as unique_commands values(CommandLine) as commands by ComputerName, User
| eval first_seen=strftime(first_seen,"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
| where unique_commands>=4
| sort -unique_commands
```

## Detection Logic
Attackers performing AD reconnaissance (net group "domain admins" /domain, dsquery user, nltest /domain_trusts) issue a burst of distinct enumeration commands from a single host/user in a short window — this catches the pattern rather than any single command.

## False Positive Considerations
IT helpdesk and sysadmins run these commands routinely — this is intentionally scoped as a low-severity enrichment signal, best used in combination with other detections rather than standalone paging.

## Tuning Notes
Feed into the SOC dashboard's 'reconnaissance activity' panel; use as supporting evidence during incident triage rather than a standalone trigger.
